{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbY3ZHd8UaoEcHpASZzw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote all three files for the repair attempt, and the candidate passes the self-check. That check doesn't test novelty, though, so I can't confirm it clears the near-duplicate gate that rejected the first attempt.\n\n**What changed.** The first attempt was rejected because its predictions almost matched `asymmetric_ideal_alternation_mirror`. This version takes a different route. It keeps everything in the incumbent `individual_alternation_sensitivity_mirror`:\n- each person's own ideal switching rate and how sharply they react to missing it\n- each person's own penalties for lopsided heads/tails counts and for the longest streak\n- the shared weights on the final streak and on mirror symmetry (palindromes)\n- each person's own guessing rate\n\nThe one change is how the penalty for missing your ideal switching rate grows. In the incumbent it grows with the square of the distance; here it grows in straight proportion to it. Put simply, every switch too many or too few costs the same. That means small misses near the ideal still count, and big misses aren't punished extra hard. The hypothesis cites the critique that survived the false-discovery correction: when both sequences alternate a lot, people pick the more alternating one more often than the incumbent predicts.\n\n- **Name:** `linear_distance_ideal_alternation_mirror`\n- **Code:** the distance is computed as `sqrt(d² + 1e-4)`, which keeps it smooth at zero. I also moved the prior on the population's average sensitivity from 2.5 to 1.5, since a straight-line distance needs a different scale. No other parameters were added.\n\n**Self-check.** It printed `OK`. The model loads, the log-probability is finite, the data contract holds, and the short one-chain smoke fit gave an ELPD-LOO of −2315.3. Two warnings remain:\n- Convergence wasn't verified, because a single chain can't report it.\n- PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable: 7.2% of trials had a high Pareto k, above the 1% tolerance. That may just reflect the 100-draw fit. If admission's full fit gets the same verdict, the model's ELPD estimate won't be trusted, which matters for pruning and for being chosen as the winner.\n\n**Risk.** I expect the straight-line penalty to predict differently enough from the incumbent and the asymmetric model to pass the novelty gate, but I didn't measure that. A second rejection is final for this slot.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_4_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2830,"cache_read_input_tokens":80361,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2830,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de6250f4-128e-4b9a-870f-55f52622fb6d","uuid":"79d15441-7b6f-42c3-bb6b-1791b82da5fb","timestamp":"2026-10-01T12:42:56.792Z","request_id":"req_011CfbY3YcwUuWx4cQ4Kot2R"}
